# MeTTa compatibility xfail list
# Format:  <relative-path>  <STATUS>:<CATEGORY>:<id>  [free-form notes]
# STATUS  ∈ COMPILE_FAIL | RUN_EXCEPTION | ASSERT_FAIL
# CATEGORY ∈ FEATURE_PENDING | STDLIB_GAP | SEMANTIC_DIVERGENCE | KNOWN_BUG
#
# A test is EXPECTED_FAIL when its actual STATUS matches the line.
# A mismatch (test fails for a different reason) is reported as REGRESSION.
# A test now passing is reported as UNEXPECTED_PASS — remove its line.
#
# The runner skips files imported by other files in the suite (modules).
# So entries here only need to cover files that run as standalone tests.

# f1_imports COMPILES and runs. The multivalued-tuple-lift blocker is FIXED: the first assert's
# `((let $x (get-atoms &self) (get-type $x)))` now lifts into
#   flat-map?(\$v. map?(\$e. ($e), ((\ ($x) (get-type $x)) $v)), (get-atoms &self))
# instead of freezing as an Atom[] holding a lambda. The `contains`/`is-space` and the first
# `import!` asserts pass too.
#
# What remains is NOT about non-determinism — it is MODULE VISIBILITY, at :56
#   !(assertEqual (match &m (= (f 2) $x) $x) (g 3))
# The match-result half is DONE (`matchReduceDeep`), and so is RUN-TIME visibility: a module's
# load-time `&self` is rebound to the module's own space, so `g`'s rule no longer reaches
# f1_imports' space before :61. Actual is now `(g (+ 1 2))`, expected computes 103. Two gaps left:
#  1. COMPILE-TIME symbol resolution is neither target- nor order-aware — `resolvedFunctions` is
#     one flat map filled from every compiled source. So the EXPECTED `(g 3)`, which must stay
#     inert here, is emitted as a static call to f1_moduleC.g and computes 103.
#     Repro: `!(import! &m f1_moduleA)` + `!(assertEqualToResult (g 3) ((g 3)))` → actual [103];
#     with no import at all the same assert passes; with the import BELOW the assert, still 103.
#  2. The reducer does not reduce the ARGUMENTS of an inert application, so the actual side stops
#     at `(g (+ 1 2))` where hyperon gives `(g 3)` — the runtime counterpart of the `(ln (+ 2 2))`
#     -> `(ln 4)` rule codegen already applies to static terms.
# Also latent, found on the way: the registry step of the reducer can invoke a compiled function
# with an argument shape its body cannot take; that now leaves the term inert instead of throwing.
f1_imports.metta            ASSERT_FAIL:FEATURE_PENDING:module-visibility-order    :56 `(g 3)` reduces to 103 (cross-module symbols ignore import order/target) and the match result is unreduced; see notes above
